                "text": "Let me understand the task. I need to create 4 demonstration pairs using show_transformed_input. The verifier will compute the output.\n\nLet me think about the rule carefully. Input 3x3, find uniform row or column, output 9x9.\n\nNote: verifier checks rows first. So if there's a uniform row, it uses row logic. I need to make inputs.\n\nWait, the rule says exactly one row or column is uniform. But I should be careful about ambiguity - the verifier checks rows first, so if a column is uniform too... Let's just make clean examples.\n\nLet me create 4 diverse examples:\n\n1. Uniform row at position 0 (top row). Output: tile input 3x side by side, in rows 0-2.\n2. Uniform row at position 1 (middle). Output in rows 3-5.\n3. Uniform row at position 2 (bottom). Output in rows 6-8.\n4. Uniform column at position 0 (left). Output: tile vertically in columns 0-2.\n5. Uniform column at position 1 (middle).\n6. Uniform column at position 2 (right).\n\nI need exactly 4. Let me pick a mix: e.g., row position 0, row position 2, column position 1, column position 2. Actually better to cover variety but only 4 allowed.\n\nHmm, wait - I need to be careful with the verifier logic. For a uniform row, output tiles input side by side (1,3). For column, tiles vertically (3,1). But note the verifier checks rows first: for each row i, if all cells in row equal row[i,0]. So the uniform row must be truly all same.\n\nLet me design inputs where only one line is uniform.\n\nExample A: uniform row 0.\nInput:\n[5,5,5]\n[1,2,3]\n[4,6,7]\n\nCheck rows: row0 all 5 -> uniform. Output: rows 0-2 = tile side by side.\n\nExample B: uniform row 2.\nInput:\n[1,2,3]\n[4,5,6]\n[8,8,8]\n\nExample C: uniform column 0.\nInput:\n[7,1,2]\n[7,3,4]\n[7,5,6]\n\nWait but the verifier checks rows first. Row0 = [7,1,2] not uniform. Row1 not uniform. Row2 not uniform. Then columns: col0 all 7 -> uniform. Good.\n\nExample D: uniform column 2.\nInput:\n[2,3,9]\n[4,5,9]\n[6,7,9]\n\nRows not uniform